BLOCK 2 — LICENSE, SECURITY, CONTAINERS AND PIPELINES

LICENSE GATE

Inspect all of the following:

- root LICENSE and COPYING files;
- source-file copyright headers;
- direct dependencies;
- transitive dependencies;
- vendored code;
- container base images;
- GitHub Actions;
- GitLab CI/CD components;
- fonts, icons, images, sounds and media;
- models, datasets and prompts;
- API terms and hosted-service terms.

Use SPDX identifiers wherever possible and retain the exact source URL and commit or release reference.

Classification:

GREEN:
Verified MIT, BSD, ISC, Apache-2.0 or similarly permissive licensing, with notices, attribution and patent requirements recorded.

YELLOW:
LGPL, MPL, CDDL, dual-license, source-available or custom licenses. Isolate and route for legal and architecture review before inclusion.

RED / QUARANTINE:
No license, ambiguous license, proprietary terms, non-commercial terms, no-derivatives terms, Commons Clause, SSPL, BSL, GPL or AGPL when incompatible with the intended distribution model, or any component whose terms cannot be verified.

A RED or YELLOW result is not a legal conclusion. It means the component cannot silently enter the commercial core. Never copy code with no verified permission. Prefer an adapter, external service boundary, clean-room implementation or a different component.

Create:

- dependency SBOM in CycloneDX or SPDX format;
- license compatibility matrix;
- third-party attribution and NOTICE material for approved components;
- vulnerability report;
- secret-detection report;
- container and base-image report;
- provenance report;
- rejected and quarantined component register.

SUPPLY-CHAIN GATE

Every external input must pass:

fork/PR/MR/archive/package/container
→ quarantine
→ checksum and provenance check
→ license check
→ dependency and SBOM scan
→ secret scan
→ SAST and malicious-pattern scan
→ isolated tests
→ architecture contract check
→ human approval where required
→ staging
→ canary
→ release
→ rollback readiness

Never execute an artifact merely to inspect it.

For every artifact, retain:

- source repository;
- source commit SHA;
- workflow or pipeline ID;
- artifact ID;
- SHA-256 digest;
- creation time;
- expiry time;
- builder identity;
- verification result;
- test result;
- promotion decision.

Pin:
- GitLab components to an immutable commit SHA or verified release;
- GitHub Actions to immutable commit SHAs;
- container images to immutable digests;
- package dependencies through lockfiles;
- model and data assets to exact versions and hashes.

Never use unreviewed `latest`, floating branches or `curl | sh` installation patterns in production.

PIPELINE GATE

Inspect the actual GitLab and GitHub pipeline files before modifying them.

Add only the smallest compatible jobs required for:

- inventory;
- license verification;
- SBOM generation;
- secret detection;
- dependency and vulnerability scanning;
- SAST;
- unit tests;
- integration tests;
- end-to-end or smoke tests;
- regression tests;
- build;
- artifact validation;
- provenance verification;
- staging and rollback checks.

If GitLab CI/CD components are used, verify their source, exact version, inputs, permissions and behavior before inclusion. Avoid job-name collisions and unexpected configuration merges.

If a pipeline cannot run because a tool, runner, permission or variable is missing, report the exact blocker. Never simulate a passing pipeline.


